    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Our lab has previously demonstrated that mouse fibroblasts can be converted into functional induced neuronal (iN) cells through the forced expression of three transcription factors Ascl1, Brn2, and Myt1. We have further shown that human fibroblasts can also be reprogrammed with additional transcription factors. However, it would be desirable to generate iN cells from adult and aged fibroblasts for use in neurological disease modeling and regenerative medicine. One major challenge is the reduced efficiency of iN cell formation in aged fibroblasts. Here we show that increased cellular senescence and p16/p19 levels, decreased proliferation, and ageing-associated FoxO3 transcription factor levels correlated with reduced reprogramming efficiency. We show that germline loss of the known Ascl1-inhibitor, and longevity-associated, transcription factor FoxO3 increased the functional maturation and reprogramming efficiency of iN cells derived from adult and aged fibroblasts. Our results indicate that the epigenetic impairment of reprogramming in aged cells can be overcome by simple genetic manipulation. The data presented here provides novel insight into iN cell reprogramming from aged fibroblasts and delineates a process to improve the reprogrammability of aged fibroblasts—a step closer towards the ultimate goal of using patient-specific iN cells in therapeutic applications and disease modeling.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
